"""register_28nov_mw2_direction.py -- TRACK 3.146: MW-1 result and its priors; MW-2 (direction and the cost of a late source) fixed BEFORE it runs; the lag measurement of the two Treasury sources. Idempotent."""
from pathlib import Path
t = Path("docs/TRACK.md"); s = t.read_text()
A = """

### 3.146 MW-1 result: auctions move all four futures; MW-2 fixed before the run: direction, and what a late source costs; the lag of Fiscal Data to be measured (28 Nov 23:30)
**MW-1, result (founder's run of 28 Nov; `docs/mw_auctions.md`).** 790 auction closings of notes and bonds since May 2018 (744 at 13:00, 43 at 11:30 New York). Futures history: 412 of 412 month-files, about US$43.50 in all (14.99 + 28.51); the second part took 152 minutes at 12 to 35 seconds a file. 11 of 16 tests pass (limit 0.0031). Size of the move after the close against the ordinary move at that minute: 10-year note future 2.79 times at 5 minutes, 2.06 at 15, 1.78 at 30, 1.41 at 60 (all pass); S&P 500 future 1.25 at 5 and 1.21 at 30 (pass), 15 and 60 no; Nasdaq-100 future 1.27 at 5 and 1.19 at 30 (pass), 15 and 60 no; gold future 1.23 at 5, 1.11 at 15, 1.13 at 30 (pass), 60 no.
**Reading.** A Treasury auction result is a market-wide headline that moves prices: the note future most, and mostly in the first minutes; shares and gold a little (about a fifth to a quarter more than an ordinary minute). Under the founder's rule of 20 Nov this kind of headline has earned its place in the listener. It gives NO direction and so no trade.
**Priors.** #252 (the note passes at 5 and 15 minutes): RIGHT. #253 (S&P 500 and Nasdaq-100 pass at no window): WRONG, both pass at 5 and 30. #254 (gold passes at no window): WRONG, it passes at 5, 15 and 30. Claude under-rated how far an auction result reaches beyond bonds.
**Founder, 28 Nov 22:38:** 'check whether moving to this "the Treasury's Fiscal Data, updates more slowly" will lag by how much amount, and then we test on how significantly does the lag affect on the trades signals. So you will also have to think of a way to see which tradeable rule we have so far can correlate with this treasury info.'
**Which tradable rule uses Treasury information today: none.** The rules on the page are the results rule and the Fed-statement rule (first hour after a company's results or a Fed statement) and the sell-off rules SS-2a/b/c (daily closes of single stocks). None reads an auction. So today a slower auction source changes no trade signal. The lag matters only for a rule built on auctions, which does not exist yet. MW-2 looks for one and measures the cost of lag inside the same test.
**(1) The lag, measured, not guessed.** `src/market_wire.py` now collects the Fiscal Data auction list next to the TreasuryDirect one, both once a minute. `python -m src.market_wire lag` pairs every result both showed while the listener ran and writes `docs/treasury_lag.md`. Bills are auctioned on most Mondays to Thursdays, so the first pairs come within days. The TreasuryDirect source stays on until the lag is known; the founder's KEEP or MOVE decision waits for it.
**(2) MW-2 (`python -m src.mw_direction run`), fixed now.** Two ways to get a direction, both from data on disk. SIGNAL A, the first move: go the way the 10-year note future went in the first 5 minutes after the close. SIGNAL B, demand: buy when the auction's bid-to-cover is above the average of the six auctions of the same term before it, sell when below. The LAG is a dimension of the grid: entry 1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 30 or 60 minutes after the close (signal A from minute 5). Holding time 15, 30, 60 minutes, or until 15:59 New York. Four futures. Seven situations: all auctions; short terms (2 to 7 years); long terms (10 to 30 years); each of the main page's four market conditions (as of the day before). Measure: average result per trade in basis points, signed by the signal, before cost. Chance: signal A against the same trade on the 20 sessions before with no auction at that minute (5,000 draws); signal B against 5,000 shuffles of the demand signs. VERDICT: one per signal on its whole grid -- how often the strongest cell of a draw is at least as strong as the strongest real cell; under 0.05 = more than luck. Single cells get no verdict.
**What would make it tradable.** More than luck on the whole grid AND an average clearly above the cost. Cost as an assumption: about one price step in and one out; one step is roughly 1.4 bp for the note future, 0.4 for the S&P 500 future, 0.2 for the Nasdaq-100 future, 0.3 for the gold future. If a signal passes, it goes to a forward record before any rule appears on the page.
**Checked on made-up data before sending:** with the note's first move made to continue for 30 minutes on auction days only, signal A was more than luck on the note (largest at entry 5, shrinking with later entries, gone by entry 60) and signal B was not more than luck. The lag report paired a made-up bill seen 47 minutes apart.
**Priors.** #255: signal A is more than luck on its grid, the strongest cells being the note future with early entries. #256: signal B is not more than luck. #257: wherever a cell is strong, it is weaker by half or more at entry 30 than at its earliest entry. #258: Fiscal Data shows a result more than 30 minutes after TreasuryDirect.
"""
if "### 3.146 MW-1 result: auctions move all four futures" not in s: t.write_text(s.rstrip("\n") + A); print("  TRACK.md: 3.146 added")
else: print("  TRACK.md: 3.146 already there -- skipped")
c = Path("docs/SOURCE_CANDIDATES.md"); y = c.read_text(); key = "Treasury auction results: TESTED 28 Nov"
row = "| Treasury auction results: TESTED 28 Nov 2026 (MW-1) | market-wide headline, first kind | 11 of 16 tests pass: the note future moves 2.8 times the ordinary in the first 5 minutes; shares and gold 1.2 to 1.3 times | moves prices, no direction; MW-2 (direction and the cost of a late source) registered in TRACK 3.146 |"
